  "image_name": "ce93fda6825abf3c7376954e56c977db.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes catalyst-design ideas and reaction strategies for electrochemical CO2 conversion, with emphasis on preventing unwanted carbonate formation. The top row shows four broad ways to tune catalysts: changing metal–metal interactions with bimetallic, high-entropy, or single-atom alloy sites; modifying metal–substrate interactions such as single metal atoms anchored in a carbon-like lattice; engineering defects through dopants, vacancies, or strain in layered materials; and tuning molecular catalysts through ligand, metal-center, and hydrophobicity control.\n\nThe lower half contrasts non-aqueous and aqueous systems. In non-aqueous media, the main problems are high charging voltage, carbonate buildup, and blockage of active sites during discharge. The proposed solutions use redox mediators or an oxalate pathway to redirect CO2 chemistry and suppress formation of carbonate plus carbon. In aqueous systems, the key issues are poor selectivity, carbonate deposition, and poor reversibility. A membrane-separated setup appears to suffer from carbonate accumulation, while two suggested remedies are tandem reactions that couple intermediates toward multicarbon products and regulation of the local environment to limit parasitic reactions involving water and carbonate. Overall, the figure links catalyst structure and reaction environment to improved CO2 electrochemistry.",
